{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3139","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3139","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3139\",\"parent_revision\":\"9a8f6d2eb4db5fc8bd8104c135aa42de1faac89f\",\"root_revision\":\"794d88ce7de64b01c89e9e4de3dacdeba556c832b71c2287683bd225e7069ae1\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: 2026-08-22\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nEvery agent definition enumerates its MCP tools by exact name in `tools:` frontmatter. This couples MCP server code to agent frontmatter in both directions, and neither direction fails loudly.\\n\\nRemove or rename a tool on a server, and every agent still listing it carries a dead entry: the entry is dropped and the rest of the grant survives, with no error. Add a tool to a server, and no agent listing tools individually can see it until every relevant `tools:` list is hand-edited \u2014 the tool is invisible rather than broken, so nothing surfaces the gap. As a maintainer, when I change an MCP server's tool set I have no signal telling me which agent definitions I have just desynchronised.\\n\\nMeasured behaviour, 2026-08-22, twelve controlled single-entry probes cross-checked against code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents and .../errors:\\n\\n- An entry resolving to no tool is dropped; the rest of the grant survives. Not an error, and it does not zero the list.\\n- If every entry resolves to nothing, the subagent refuses to launch with a named error grouping entries by cause. It does not launch tool-less.\\n- A server-level pattern works and grants exactly that server's tools: `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__*` and the bare `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog` each returned that server's 46 tools.\\n- A server pattern grants tools only while that server is connected \u2014 the identical definition returned 46 tools with the server up and refused to launch with it down.\\n\\nTwo instances already present in the tree, both silent drops:\\n\\n- `artifact_migrate` appears in the `tools:` list of 14 agent files. It does not exist on the backlog server \u2014 verified against `backlog_core/server.py` and two repo tests that assert its absence.\\n- Eleven MCP servers are referenced across agent definitions but are not connected in this checkout: `mcp__exa__`, `mcp__context7__`, `mcp__git-forensics__`, `mcp__github__`, a non-local `mcp__Ref__`, and others. `ecosystem-researcher` is written to depend on a firecrawl server it can never receive, and its body treats those tools as required rather than optional.\\n\\nImpact: an agent silently loses a capability its own definition claims it has, and behaves as though the tool were simply unavailable. The failure surfaces only as degraded agent output, at runtime, in whatever install it lands in. An MCP-only grant additionally carries a hard runtime dependency on that server being connected.\\n\\nRelates to #2292 (consolidating the backlog server's flat tool list into action-based tools would shrink the surface this problem spans) and #3094 (audit of missing `Bash` access in agent `tools:` lists \u2014 the same class of frontmatter/capability mismatch).\\n\\nCorrection to an earlier revision of this item: it claimed a server-scoped wildcard \\\"fails open\\\" and granted the full tool set, and concluded there was no safe escape from hand-maintaining enumerated lists. Both are false. That claim came from a single four-entry multi-variable probe; the twelve single-variable probes above supersede it. Server-level grants are a working group mechanism and are the likely direction for reducing this coupling.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **agent tools: frontmatter drifts from mcp server tool lists with no enforcement and silent failure in both directions** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 measured 2026-08-22 while scoping the dispatch-contract work\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-22\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"ee7a7406bf239dbb301a1d5ebe8862ec3b0a048d9776115a358cd852bafc1670\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQOH7PA\"}"}